n_engagement&amp;utm_source=post_link&amp;post_id=166949319\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><!--[if mso | IE]><span style=\"mso-text-raise:-2px;\"><![endif]--><img src=\"https://cdn-web-assets.dyspatch.io/org_01gvh8mwwb22j90stngkwjbkvx/fdr_01jmdv6s5fevecq2mwr1tmmwss%2FmEPm9PuPRYKykZNvWcmk_HeartOutline.png\" width=\"19\" alt=\"like\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><!--[if mso | IE]></span><![endif]--></a></td></tr></tbody></table>\n </td>\n <td>\n <table><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"https://frienji.kenjilopezalt.com/posts/konbini-taste-166949319?utm_medium=newsletter_post_email&amp;utm_campaign=patron_engagement&amp;utm_source=post_link&amp;post_id=166949319\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><!--[if mso | IE]><span style=\"mso-text-raise:-2px;\"><![endif]--><img src=\"https://cdn-web-assets.dyspatch.io/org_01gvh8mwwb22j90stngkwjbkvx/fdr_01jmdv6s5fevecq2mwr1tmmwss%2FpONe1AzqSS2ydddAswCs_BubbleOutlined%20(1).png\" width=\"19\" alt=\"comment\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><!--[if mso | IE]></span><![endif]--></a></td></tr></tbody></table>\n </td>\n <td>\n <table><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"https://frienji.kenjilopezalt.com/posts/konbini-taste-166949319?utm_medium=newsletter_post_email&amp;utm_campaign=patron_engagement&amp;utm_source=post_link&amp;post_id=166949319\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><!--[if mso | IE]><span style=\"mso-text-raise:-2px;\"><![endif]--><img src=\"https://cdn-web-assets.dyspatch.io/org_01gvh8mwwb22j90stngkwjbkvx/fdr_01jmdv6s5fevecq2mwr1tmmwss%2FFkHQyZCfR8O5oErCuqC4_Share%20(2).png\" width=\"19\" alt=\"share\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><!--[if mso | IE]></span><![endif]--></a></td></tr></tbody></table>\n </td>\n <td>\n \n <table><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"https://open.patreon.com/home?utm_id=1bb4071c-9172-4b5e-bcb9-21a27de9cb12&amp;utm_medium=email\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View in app</a></td></tr></tbody></table>\n \n \n <!--[if !mso]--><table><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"https://open.patreon.com/home?utm_id=1bb4071c-9172-4b5e-bcb9-21a27de9cb12&amp;utm_medium=email\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View in app</a></td></tr></tbody></table><!--[endif]-->\n \n </td>\n </tr>\n </tbody>\n </table>\n \n </td></tr>\n </tbody></table><!--[if mso | IE]>\n</td></tr></table>\n<![endif]--></td></tr></tbody></table><!--[if mso | IE]>\n</td></tr></table>\n<![endif]-->\n \n <!--[if mso | IE]>\n<table align=\"center\" border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" class=\"row-mb-pd-outlook\" style=\"width:600px;\" width=\"600\"><tr><td style=\"line-height:0px;font-size:0px;mso-line-height-rule:exactly;\">\n<![endif]--><table><tbody><tr><td><!--[if mso | IE]>\n<table role=\"presentation\" border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"vertical-align:top;width:576px;\">\n<![endif]--><table>\n <tbody><tr><td><h3><em>Konbini are the cornerstones (quite literally) of quick, convenient food and drink throughout Japan. Kenji put the big three—7-Eleven, FamilyMart, and Lawson—to the test!</em></h3><p>Hi Frienjis, </p><p>It’s Erin, Content &amp; Community Manager here on Patreon with another episode of <strong>Kenji Eats Japan.</strong> This time, it’s a battle for the ages. It’s a <strong>Konbini Taste Test!</strong></p><a href=\"https://frienji.kenjilopezalt.com/collection/2272438?view=expanded\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">\n        <table>\n        <tbody>\n          <tr>\n            <td>\n                <img src=\"https://c10.patreonusercontent.com/4/patreon-media/p/post/166949319/9ebecfc97fea48fc8d910d6c643577e6/eyJhIjoxLCJ3Ijo4MjB9/1.png?token-hash=ZHU0ahGLTix6jKCSd85TpM8-LE_wo0jANXMj_T0Rjgw%3D&amp;token-time=1788393600\" width=\"592\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\">\n            </td>\n          </tr>\n        </tbody>\n      </table>\n        </a><h2>But First... What’s A Konbini?</h2><p>Well, what does it sound like? Konbini is actually a <strong>shortened phrase for “konbiniensu sutoru” (meaning convenience store) </strong>in Japanese. It sounds similar to English because it’s one of many loanwords originating<em> from</em> English that the Japanese language utilizes, just like kurejitto kādo (credit card), eakon (air conditioning), or hankachi (handkerchief).</p><a href=\"https://frienji.kenjilopezalt.com/posts/konbini-taste-166949319?utm_medium=newsletter_post_email&amp;utm_campaign=patron_engagement&amp;utm_source=post_link&amp;post_id=166949319&amp;media_id=720903297\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">\n        <table>\n        <tbody>\n          <tr>\n            <td>\n                <img src=\"https://c10.patreonusercontent.com/4/patreon-media/p/post/166949319/1a78be0a751f4a24bf6ba207f46a1536/eyJhIjoxLCJ3Ijo4MjB9/1.JPG?token-hash=yfBEkZifjXGhJTzdHAg-exfnfLMss9_5o0kmFAIm_EI%3D&amp;token-time=1788393600\" width=\"592\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\">\n            </td>\n          </tr>\n        </tbody>\n      </table>\n        </a><p>Lawson dressed up in Hiroshima Carp colors outside their baseball stadium. Photo by Lena Davidson, Kenji's head of operations.</p><h2>Convenience Culture</h2><p><strong>Japan loves a convenient food choice. </strong>Going into our team’s trip earlier this year, I knew the country had a big vending machine and konbini culture. The moment I stepped foot in Kyoto I realized, oh... they are quite literally on every block and every corner, sometimes in multiple amounts. The numbers back it up: Across Japan, there are around 60,000 konbini shops and 4–5 million vending machines (that’s one machine for every 30–40 residents)! <strong><a href=\"https://www.timeout.com/tokyo/shopping/japan-convenience-stores-konbini-are-the-best-in-the-world\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">¹</a></strong>  <strong><a href=\"https://www.japan-guide.com/e/e2010.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">²</a></strong></p><p>The <strong>vending machines</strong> come in all shapes and forms. Some sell your expected selection of soda, tea, and other sugary drinks while others might focus exclusively on coffee, beer, or even fresh-pressed orange juice. Some vending machines sell hot food and some sell frozen, microwavable meals so you can pop across the street and grab a dish for dinner or dessert. And while I didn’t spot any of these myself, some sell fresh produce, disaster relief items, collectibles, and more.<strong><a href=\"https://en.japantravel.com/article/only-in-japan-vending-machines-that-go-way-beyond-drinks/71451\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">³</a></strong> And I’m not even including the <strong><a href=\"https://guidable.co/culture/gachapon-a-unique-japanese-culture-you-should-not-miss/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gashapon craze</a></strong> in all this, though you can spot a fair number of those in the background of today's video!</p><p>While vending machines were surely part of our team’s travels—an ice cold Pocari Sweat is a great accompaniment while <strong><a href=\"https://youtu.be/nXbTy2IIpxA?si=wVJPI4L42OFDOAtC&amp;t=1552\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">climbing a mountain to see some monkeys</a></strong>—we stayed <strong>focused on the konbini</strong> for the sake of this taste test.</p><a href=\"https://frienji.kenjilopezalt.com/posts/konbini-taste-166949319?utm_medium=newsletter_post_email&amp;utm_campaign=patron_engagement&amp;utm_source=post_link&amp;post_id=166949319&amp;media_id=720904514\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">\n        <table>\n        <tbody>\n          <tr>\n            <td>\n                <img src=\"https://c10.patreonusercontent.com/4/patreon-media/p/post/166949319/a5f61e1d4ce142dd9d7b8feb45fdda72/eyJhIjoxLCJ3Ijo4MjB9/1.png?token-hash=08nJq4hUcXBH5D54zYYUTpWkk7O74wSVBYdGvscf4gE%3D&amp;token-time=1788393600\" width=\"592\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\">\n            </td>\n          </tr>\n        </tbody>\n      </table>\n        </a><p>Common konbini offerings—including Mt. Rainier coffee, a popular, ready-to-drink coffee company named after Washington's beloved mountain!</p><h2>The Big Three</h2><p>Konbini are a cornerstone of the convenient food culture that’s endemic to this country. In 2025, convenience stores set a record ¥12.05 trillion ($75.8 billion) in sales, driven by the big three: 7-Eleven, Lawson, and Family Mart.<strong><a href=\"https://www.japantimes.co.jp/business/2026/01/21/companies/convenience-store-sales-2025/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">⁴</a></strong></p><ol><li><p><strong>7-Eleven</strong> is the biggest konbini chain in the country with over 20,000 stores.<strong><a href=\"https://www.byfood.com/blog/culture/guide-to-konbini-japanese-convenience-stores\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">⁵</a></strong> Yes, it’s the same 7-Eleven we have back in the States. The chain began in Dallas in 1927 but today is actually owned by the Seven-Eleven Japan Co., Ltd. parent company.<strong><a href=\"https://www.sej.co.jp/company/en/c_profile.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">⁶</a></strong> In Japan, 7-Eleven is famous for their<strong> </strong><strong><a href=\"https://frienji.kenjilopezalt.com/posts/konbini-style-166707773\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">egg salad sandos</a></strong> and onigiri, amongst other savory food items.</p></li><li><p><strong>FamilyMart</strong> is close behind with around 17,000 stores and holds their fame in Fami-Chiki and comfortable sock stock.<strong><a href=\"https://www.family.co.jp/english/company/store.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">⁷</a></strong> The Fami-Chiki is a juicy, crispy, fried chicken thigh kept in a hot case and served in a fittingly convenient <strong><a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DXTWixqFZ1V/?utm_source=ig_web_copy_link&amp;igsh=NTc4MTIwNjQ2YQ==\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">rips-right-down-the-center wrapper</a></strong> to keep the grease off your fingers. The socks are self-explanatory, being cheap and comfy with a stylish colorway inspired by the store’s iconic blue and green logo. </p></li><li><p>Finally, there’s <strong>Lawson </strong>stores, with around 14,000 locations.<strong><a href=\"https://www.lawson.jp/en/store/japan/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">⁸</a></strong> They’re famous for their baked goods with affordable and incredibly high quality fluffy roll cakes, melonpan, and cheesecakes luring customers in. I only came across a few Lawson locations during my time there, which makes sense seeing as the areas we stayed in were <strong><a href=\"https://conbini.kikkia.dev/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">FamilyMart strongholds (according to Conbini Wars).</a></strong> </p></li></ol><a href=\"https://frienji.kenjilopezalt.com/posts/konbini-taste-166949319?utm_medium=newsletter_post_email&amp;utm_campaign=patron_engagement&amp;utm_source=post_link&amp;post_id=166949319&amp;media_id=720905723\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">\n        <table>\n        <tbody>\n          <tr>\n            <td>\n                <img src=\"https://c10.patreonusercontent.com/4/patreon-media/p/post/166949319/7c2bda834cf34439997ff7a81a86d231/eyJhIjoxLCJ3Ijo4MjB9/1.png?token-hash=e1BJKe4fN2yDR5nyhuxoxtmELuoS8cJuTJmIF7QH2bc%3D&amp;token-time=1788393600\" width=\"592\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\">\n            </td>\n          </tr>\n        </tbody>\n      </table>\n        </a><p>FamilyMart glowing! Photo by Lena Davidson.</p><h2>Taste Test!</h2><p>For this taste test, Kenji set out to try a variety of offerings from each store, highlighting their strengths and weaknesses. Our categories included egg salad sandos; onigiri; boiled eggs; fried chicken thighs (à la Fami-Chiki); socks; machine-made iced lattes; and a dessert wildcard. </p><p>The results were not what we expected… Watch the full video for the taste test and beware of a <strong>spoiler alert</strong> <strong>for the final results ahead:</strong></p><a href=\"https://frienji.kenjilopezalt.com/posts/konbini-taste-166949319?utm_medium=newsletter_post_email&amp;utm_campaign=patron_engagement&amp;utm_source=post_link&amp;post_id=166949319&amp;media_id=720895202\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">\n        <table>\n        <tbody>\n          <tr>\n            <td>\n                <img src=\"https://c10.patreonusercontent.com/4/patreon-media/p/post/166949319/816744a4b08e4944a96b76165a4cf942/eyJhIjoxLCJ3Ijo4MjB9/1.png?token-hash=9bNl_Vt2mPjmEDLzZxvef65PUcpKKXO4Zmdl3X1k7-0%3D&amp;token-time=1788393600\" width=\"592\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\">\n            </td>\n          </tr>\n        </tbody>\n      </table>\n        </a><p>Thanks for continuing to follow along with the <strong><a href=\"https://frienji.kenjilopezalt.com/collection/2272438?view=expanded\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Kenji Eats Japan series!</a></strong> Right after we filmed this taste test, the team headed to the train station to catch a Shinkansen up to Niigata prefecture to begin the business half of the trip. Stay tuned for some super sharp stories about metalworking and knife making in Tsubame-Sanjō.</p><p>— Erin, Community &amp; Content Manager</p><h2><strong><a href=\"https://frienji.kenjilopezalt.com/collection/2272438?view=expanded\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">🔗 Watch the full Kenji Eats Japan series.</a></strong></h2></td></tr>\n </tbody></table><!--[if mso | IE]>\n</td></tr></table>\n<![endif]--></td></tr></tbody></table><!--[if mso | IE]>\n</td></tr></table>\n<![endif]-->\n\n \n \n <!--[if mso | IE]>\n<table align=\"center\" border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" class=\"row-mb-pd-outlook\" style=\"width:600px;\" width=\"600\"><tr><td style=\"line-height:0px;font-size:0px;mso-line-height-rule:exactly;\">\n<![endif]--><table><tbody><tr><td><!--[if mso | IE]>\n<table role=\"presentation\" border=\"0\" c